<section id="lab-view" class="model-lab" hidden aria-labelledby="lab-title">
  <header class="lab-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">A language model, small enough to see inside</p>
    <h2 id="lab-title">Teach it text.<br /><span class="muted">Watch it guess what comes next.</span></h2>
    <p>No downloaded model. No server doing the thinking. A tiny transformer starts with random numbers and learns from your text, here in your browser. Its writing will be rough. That’s part of the lesson.</p>
    <nav class="lab-route" aria-label="Lab steps"><a href="#lab-text">01 · Give examples</a><span>→</span><a href="#lab-learn">02 · Learn patterns</a><span>→</span><a href="#lab-write">03 · Try a guess</a><span>→</span><a href="#lab-inside">04 · Look inside</a></nav>
  </header>
  <noscript><p>This experiment needs JavaScript. You can still read the Journey and explore the lessons below.</p></noscript>
  <div class="lab-section" id="lab-text">
    <div class="lab-section-title"><span>01</span><h3>First, give it something to learn from.</h3></div>
    <p>Every character is a <strong class="lab-purple">token</strong> here: a letter, a space, even a line break. The task is always the same: given some characters, guess the next one.</p>
    <label for="lab-corpus">Training text <small>160–50,000 characters · up to 128 distinct characters</small></label>
    <textarea id="lab-corpus" rows="6" spellcheck="false" maxlength="100000"></textarea>
    <div class="lab-actions"><button id="lab-example" type="button">Use cat / car / can example</button><button id="lab-prepare" class="lab-primary" type="button">Build my models</button><span id="lab-text-size"></span></div>
    <p class="lab-note">Your text stays in this tab’s memory. Reloading clears the models. Editing the text resets them. The example repeats on purpose; try your own writing to see its limits.</p>
    <div id="lab-tokens" class="lab-token-strip" aria-label="First characters and their numeric IDs"></div>
    <p id="lab-data-summary" class="lab-note"></p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab-section" id="lab-learn">
    <div class="lab-section-title"><span>02</span><h3>Two ways to make a guess.</h3></div>
    <div class="lab-columns">
      <div><h4>Count what followed.</h4><p>The counting model remembers pairs. After <code>a</code>, how often did <code>t</code>, <code>r</code>, or <code>n</code> appear? It’s ready as soon as we count the text, but sees only one character back.</p><p class="lab-note">No repeated training needed. A useful baseline to compare against.</p></div>
      <div><h4 class="lab-purple">Learn a shared recipe.</h4><p>The transformer sees up to <strong>16 characters</strong>. Its <strong>weights</strong> are adjustable numbers in a calculation. It guesses, checks the actual next character in your text, and adjusts those numbers to make that answer more likely.</p><p class="lab-note" id="lab-model-summary">One attention head · one transformer block · 16 numbers per character</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="lab-training-loop"><span>Read a short passage</span><b>→</b><span>Guess each next character</span><b>→</b><span>Measure the mistake</span><b>→</b><span>Adjust the weights</span><b>↻</b></div>
    <div class="lab-actions"><button id="lab-train" class="lab-primary" type="button" disabled>Train the transformer</button><button id="lab-pause" type="button" disabled>Pause</button><button id="lab-reset" type="button" disabled>Reset learning</button><span id="lab-step">0 / 400 updates</span></div>
    <p id="lab-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Start with the example, or paste your own text. Then build the models.</p>
    <div class="lab-loss" hidden>
      <div class="lab-loss-key"><span class="lab-orange">● Training mistake <b id="lab-train-loss">—</b></span><span class="lab-teal">● Held-out mistake <b id="lab-val-loss">—</b></span></div>
      <svg id="lab-loss-chart" viewBox="0 0 700 130" role="img" aria-label="Loss during training. Lower means better predictions on the measured passages."></svg>
      <p class="lab-note">“Loss” measures how surprised the model is by the correct answer. Lower is better. Teal uses the last 10% of your text, which is never used for weight updates. Each line measures eight fixed passages; repeated text can make this an easy test, so a falling line doesn’t prove it understands language.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="lab-section" id="lab-write">
    <div class="lab-section-title"><span>03</span><h3>Give it a start. Let it continue.</h3></div>
    <p>A model returns <strong class="lab-blue">chances</strong>, not a sentence. Pick one character using those chances, add it to the text, and repeat. You can try the untrained transformer too.</p>
    <label for="lab-prompt">Starting text</label><input id="lab-prompt" value="the ca" maxlength="200" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
    <div class="lab-settings"><label for="lab-model">Generate with<select id="lab-model"><option value="neural">Tiny transformer</option><option value="count">Counting model</option></select></label><label for="lab-temperature">Randomness <output id="lab-temperature-value">1.0</output><input id="lab-temperature" type="range" min="0.2" max="2" step="0.1" value="1" /></label></div>
    <p class="lab-note">Lower randomness favors the strongest guesses; higher randomness spreads the chances. It changes sampling, not learning.</p>
    <div class="lab-actions"><button id="lab-inspect" type="button" disabled>Show next guess</button><button id="lab-next" type="button" disabled>Add one character</button><button id="lab-generate" class="lab-primary" type="button" disabled>Generate 80 characters</button><button id="lab-stop" type="button" disabled>Stop</button><button id="lab-clear" type="button" disabled>Back to starting text</button></div>
    <div class="lab-output" aria-label="Generated text"><span id="lab-seed">the ca</span><mark id="lab-generated"></mark><span class="lab-caret" aria-hidden="true">▎</span></div>
    <p class="lab-note" id="lab-generation-note">This treats the text as a continuous stream, with no special end token. Generation stops at the requested length. Training and generation run separately so you can inspect a stable model.</p>
    <div id="lab-prediction" hidden>
      <p class="lab-snapshot">A snapshot <strong>before</strong> the next character: <code id="lab-context"></code><br /><span id="lab-choice"></span></p>
      <div class="lab-columns"><div><h4>Counting model <small>last character only</small></h4><div id="lab-count-bars"></div><p id="lab-count-note" class="lab-note"></p></div><div><h4 class="lab-blue">Transformer <small id="lab-prediction-step"></small></h4><div id="lab-neural-bars"></div><p class="lab-note">Top six raw model chances, before adjusting randomness. All characters still participate in sampling.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="lab-section" id="lab-inside">
    <div class="lab-section-title"><span>04</span><h3>Follow the numbers behind that guess.</h3></div>
    <p id="lab-inside-empty">Build the models, then show a guess. These diagrams will show the transformer’s real calculations for that passage.</p>
    <div id="lab-internals" hidden>
      <h4>Characters → IDs → embeddings</h4>
      <p>An ID tells us which character we have; its size says nothing about meaning. An <strong class="lab-purple">embedding</strong> replaces that ID with 16 adjustable numbers. The model can reuse these numbers across different passages instead of storing a separate rule for every passage.</p>
      <div id="lab-embedding" class="lab-scroll"></div>
      <p class="lab-note">Each row is a real embedding lookup. Purple = positive, orange = negative; stronger color = larger magnitude relative to this snapshot. These dimensions aren’t labeled ideas like “animal” or “speed.” They start random and are learned together with the other weights.</p>
      <details><summary>How does it use those numbers?</summary><p>First, it adds a learned position vector to each embedding, so order matters. Then it multiplies and adds numbers using its weights to produce queries, keys, and values. Query–key matches determine attention: how much of each earlier position’s value vector to mix in.</p></details>
      <h4 class="lab-teal">Attention: which positions contribute?</h4>
      <p>The last row belongs to the last character in the passage. It mixes information from the positions shown here before predicting what comes next. Select a cell to read its share. Blank future cells are blocked, so the model can’t peek at the answer.</p>
      <div id="lab-attention" class="lab-scroll"></div>
      <p id="lab-attention-detail" class="lab-note">Each row adds up to 100%. Attention shows one mixing operation, not a complete explanation of the prediction.</p>
      <div class="lab-training-loop"><span class="lab-purple">Embeddings + positions</span><b>→</b><span class="lab-teal">Attention mixes context</span><b>→</b><span>Small neural network</span><b>→</b><span class="lab-blue">One score per character</span><b>→</b><span>Chances</span></div>
      <p>A small neural network transforms the mixed numbers, while residual connections carry earlier information forward. A final weighted calculation makes one score for every character. <strong>Softmax</strong> turns those scores into the chances you saw above.</p>
      <p>During training, the actual next character supplies the answer. <strong>Backpropagation</strong> calculates how changing each weight would change the loss; the optimizer uses those gradients to adjust the weights, including the embeddings. Generation only uses the resulting recipe. It does not train it.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="lab-note">This is a real, tiny language model, not a large language model or a chatbot. More fluent systems need much more data, capacity, and training. <a href="/?view=journey#concepts">Follow the Journey →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
